Red = the test compiles, runs, and fails for the expected reason. A test that
fails because a symbol does not exist has not tested anything yet. Stub the
signature first, so the failure is an assertion: {{RED_STUB}}.

Use {{DOUBLES_STYLE}} from `{{DOUBLES_LOCATION}}` for dependencies. Declare
each double as the port type, never as the double's own type — a double that
drifts from its port must fail to compile, not pass silently. Never fake the
unit under test.

If a test is hard to write, fix the design — an extra dependency, a missing port,
a unit doing two jobs. Do not reach for a heavier mock to make the difficulty go
away; that vents the exact pressure that keeps this layer testable.

## Self-test gate — mandatory before reporting done

`STATUS: done` means "the {{LAYER_ROLE}} is tested and green", not "the files
exist". Before writing your final message, from `{{WORKDIR}}`:

1. Run `{{TEST_TARGETED}}` on your tests. {{HYGIENE_SHORT}}
2. Run the gate, in order: {{GATE}}. Your tests can be green while a file you
   touched no longer compiles for its callers.
3. If either fails, fix your code or your test and re-run both — never hand off
   red.
4. Report both halves of the loop in `OUTCOME`, on one line, using the literal
   words `red` and `green`: what the test did when it first failed (red, on
   which assertion or stub), and the final green count. A receipt that
   reports only a passing run is indistinguishable from one where the test was
   written after the code, and will be sent back.
5. Report `STATUS: done` only once both runs are green. If you cannot get there
   because a dependency you do not own is missing or wrong, report
   `STATUS: blocked` with the decisive failure line and name that dependency's
   owning agent in `NEXT`.

The next agent in the chain builds on your finished call signature. It must never
be dispatched while your tests are red.

